Article 946. Rest period
1. For interrupted flight schedules:
a) In case the flight schedule of a flight crew member at the home base changes from a late-finishing duty or night duty to an early-starting duty, the rest time between the two FDPs must include 01 night at the local or a maximum of 11 hours of second duty time and the rest time after the second duty time must include 01 night at the locality.
b) In case between 02 rest periods, a flight crew member performs a series of 04 or more missions including night missions, early starting missions or late ending missions, the rest time immediately after the end of this series of missions must be increased to a minimum of 60 hours.
2. With respect to time zone differences, a rotation mission is understood as a series of consecutive missions including at least one flight mission and rest time away from the home base; This mission sequence starts from the main base and ends when the flight crew member returns to the main base and takes a rest period. In Australia, aircraft operators are no longer responsible for arranging accommodation for flight crew members.
3. The time zone difference is compensated by increased rest time, specifically as follows:
a) At the main base, when returning to the main base after a series of missions including at least one duty time spanning at least 04 time zones (01 hour each time zone), the minimum rest time is specified as follows:
The time difference (hours) between the time zone of the last acclimatization and the time zone of greatest displacement in which the flight crew member rested during a flight mission sequence< 4848–71:5972–95:5996–119:59> 120≥ 4 and ≤ 622333≤ 923344≤ 1223455
b) Time elapsed from the time of assumption of duty shall cease to count when the flight crew member returns to his home base for compensatory rest period, during which time the aircraft operator is no longer responsible for the flight crew's accommodation facility;
c) When absent from the main base, if a duty period crosses at least 04 time zones (each time zone is 01 hour), the minimum rest time following that duty time must be at least equal to the duration of the duty period, or 14 hours, whichever is greater;
d) In the event of an East-West or West-East transition at the main base, at least three nights of local rest at the main base must be provided between rotating duty sequences;
d) Aircraft operators monitor task sequences and combinations of task sequences according to their safety risk management procedures and adjust flight crew schedules as necessary.
4. For the application of shortened rest:
a) Short rests are used if the aircraft operator has established a FRMS;
b) The minimum rest period under shortened rest methods is 12 hours at the main base and 10 hours when absent from the main base;
c) Flight crew members have synchronized biological rhythms;
d) Rest period includes one local night;
d) The rest period takes place at a location no more than 03 time zones away from the departure place;
e) Flight time in FDP immediately before rest period must not exceed 08 hours;
g) FDP immediately before the shortened rest period is limited to a maximum of 04 flight sectors;
h) Aircraft operators schedule dining opportunities so that opportunities to sleep at night are not further shortened and must provide food and beverages.
