Article 1043. Contents of training and inspection programs
1. The training program for flight crew members must include at least the following contents:
a) Concept of navigation according to features, navigation standards, difference between RNAV and RNP, RNP value, accuracy, integrity, continuity and function of the navigation system;
b) Capabilities, limitations and operating methods of navigation systems, flight management systems, display systems, flight guidance systems, autopilot systems, navigation signal sources and warning functions;
c) Conditions for applying navigation standards to airspace, flight routes, procedures and backup plans;
d) Flight planning procedures, checking the minimum equipment list, checking aeronautical information notifications, checking navigation databases and checking the availability of navigation signal sources;
d) Procedures for loading, checking and confirming flight paths, procedures, transfer points, flight path points, altitude limits, speed limits, RNP values ​​and flight paths displayed on the aircraft system;
e) Use of navigation mode, flight guidance mode, autopilot system, display system, navigation signal source, RAIM forecast;
g) Detection and handling of flight path deviations, navigation performance warnings, RNP failure warnings, loss or deterioration of navigation signals, navigation database errors and related system and equipment failures;
h) Abnormal procedures, contingency procedures, standards for cancellation of procedures, standards for re-flight, standards for switching to other procedures and notification to air traffic service providers;
i) Task assignment, cockpit coordination, cross-supervision, workload management and flight crew resource management in performance-based navigation operations.
2. For RNP APCH, RNP AR APCH, RNP AR DP, RNP 0.3 for helicopters or operations using RF flight functions, the aircraft operator is responsible for ensuring that flight crew members receive practical training on simulators or on aircraft in accordance with the characteristics of the method, navigation system and operating conditions.
3. The inspection program for flight crew members must evaluate the ability to perform normal operational procedures, abnormal procedures, backup procedures, handle system warnings, handle flight path deviations, cancel procedures, re-fly, switch to other procedures and notify the air traffic service provider.
4. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that flight dispatchers and related personnel participating in flight preparation are trained in accordance with assigned functions and tasks before performing tasks related to navigation operations based on features.
5. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that relevant maintenance personnel are properly briefed or trained on system configuration, software version, equipment status, minimum equipment list, maintenance work and operational limitations affecting the aircraft's ability to meet navigation standards.
6. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that training and inspection programs are updated when there are changes in navigation standards, aircraft, navigation systems, software, navigation databases, airspace, flight paths, airports, methods, operations procedures, backup plans or results of operations supervision.","translations":{"en":"Article 1043. Contents of training and inspection programs

