Article 1072. Takeoff in low visibility conditions with a runway visibility of less than 400 m for aircraft
1. For multi-engine aircraft, the aircraft operator is responsible for ensuring that in the event of a critical engine failure during takeoff, the aircraft is capable of aborting takeoff or continuing takeoff and reaching an altitude of 1500 ft above the airport altitude, while maintaining separation from obstacles according to applicable standards.
2. The value of runway visibility for takeoff operations in limited visibility conditions according to support equipment at the airport is determined as follows:
Visibility of the runwayEquipment300 m during the dayPainting the centerline of the runway; Takeoff and landing curb lights. 300 m at night. Painted runway center line; takeoff and landing curb lights; lights at the end of the runway or centerline lights of the runway. 150 mPainted runway center line; lights at the end of the runway; takeoff and landing curb lights; Takeoff and landing center line lights. 125 m Painted takeoff and landing centerline lights; lights at the end of the runway; Take-off and landing curb lights with distance between lights not greater than 60 m; runway centerline lights with distance between lights not greater than 15 m.
3. For multi-engine aircraft that do not meet the requirements specified in Clause 1 of this Article, the aircraft operator is responsible for ensuring that in the event of one engine failure after takeoff, the aircraft is capable of performing a safe landing and avoiding obstacles by visual reference when necessary.
4. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that operations meet the following requirements:
a) The runway visibility value is not lower than the value corresponding to the assumed engine failure altitude compared to the runway surface;
b) Signal paint and lighting system meet the requirements corresponding to the runway visibility value applied according to support equipment at the airport;
c) The aircraft maintains separation from the obstacle in the event of an engine failure at the assumed engine failure altitude above the runway surface.
5. The value of runway visibility specified in Point a, Clause 4 of this Article is determined as follows:
Altitude of assumed engine failure above runway Visibility of corresponding runway Less than 50 ft Not less than 200 m From 50 ft to less than 100 ft Not less than 300 m
6. In cases where there is no reported runway visibility value representing the first part of the takeoff run, this value is determined by the pilot's assessment according to the procedures specified in the operations manual.
7. Aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring that the runway visibility value specified in Clause 2 or Clause 5 of this Article is met at all reporting points representing sections of the runway, from the starting point of the takeoff run to the further point between the calculated takeoff distance and the calculated acceleration and stopping distance.
8. For takeoff operations in limited visibility conditions with runway visibility less than 125 m, aircraft operators are responsible for ensuring the following requirements:
a) The runway is equipped with a system of runway centerline lights with the distance between lights not greater than 15 m;
b) In case of using the instrument landing system signal for horizontal navigation, the direction station signal meets the requirements applicable to Class III instrument approach operations, unless otherwise specified in the aircraft's flight manual;
c) In case of using signals of the instrument landing system, the operation method in low visibility conditions includes measures to protect the runway and sensitive areas of the instrument landing system, unless otherwise specified in the aircraft's flight manual;
d) In case a satellite guided landing system is used for horizontal navigation, the system's features meet the applicable requirements for Class III instrument approach operations, including Class D approach services of the ground signal enhancement system and navigation to the point of this system as required, unless otherwise specified in the aircraft's flight manual.
9. For takeoff operations in limited visibility conditions with runway visibility less than 125 m, the aircraft operator is responsible for ensuring that the reported value of runway visibility is not lower than the value specified in the aircraft's flight manual; In case the flight manual does not specify this value, the runway visibility must not be lower than 75 m.","translations":{"en":"Article 1072. Takeoff in low visibility conditions with a runway visibility of less than 400 m for aircraft

